00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.550 align:start position:0%
 
diplomats from russia ukraine germany  and france are trying to resolve the  crisis over ukraine the u.s meantime is  talking tough on sanctions to discourage  russia from invading ukraine and of  course this comes as more than 100 000  russian troops hold at the ukrainian  border and with the face-off between  political leaders getting all the  attention what do the ukrainian people  make of this ramp up intentions here to  share some insights is adrian  karatnitsky board member of the  ukrainian institute of america and  contributing editor of american purpose  he formerly headed the u.s human rights  group freedom house adrian thank you so  much for being here let's get into it  right ukrainian leaders are portraying  tensions on the border as par for the  course are ukrainians interpreting it  that way or are tensions beginning to  escalate at all  in general i think ukrainians are  staying calm  and not panicking but there has been a  little bit of movement the currency has  depreciated seven or eight percent in  the last few weeks while maintaining  stability throughout the last year and  you have to keep in mind that since  april there have been roughly between 80  and 130 000 troops on ukraine's eastern  borders massed potentially for an  onslaught and the the country has  accepted this as a normal thing because  they've already  been a part of a war where 15 000 nearly  15 000 people have died so i think that  both the idea that russia is a threat  and that russia is massed at the borders  has become unfortunately a routine part  of ukraine's life  yeah it's part of the daily normal and  it's something that's disturbing that  it's become part of the daily normal um  this relative calm i guess you could  call it that ukrainians might feel  how much of it  emanates from the leadership at the  moment and i wonder to what extent that  is an economic strategy you know keep  the markets calm keep people calm  or is it a move to  avoid panic and avoid provoking moscow  to making a rash move somehow  well i think it's a combination of these  things no leader would like to set off  a panic and in fact ukraine's resilience  in the face of this makes it easier i  think i i don't think it's so much the  president although he has made  reassuring comments and the leaders of  the defense ministry and the national  security council have similarly  downplayed the imminence of the threat  but i really think it's the ukrainian  people who are  calm cool and collected uh and i think  you see it in various ways number one  there isn't any  anti-russian hysteria there are many  russians who are intermarried with  ukrainians there's no ethnic tension uh  people interact in ukrainian and in  russian and ukrainians accept the idea  that they're a  multinational country in which of course  the overwhelming majority of people are  ukrainian citizens but i think that the  atmosphere is pretty relaxed for a  country that in effect has been in a  wartime footing and face this threat for  eight years on it  right but it's kind of a case of we've  seen this all before i mean if this is  the norm for the russian ukrainian  relationship at least domestically for  myself and my friends it kind of feels  like we know this tension has been going  on but what was the singular moment what  was the impetus in the history of this  relationship between these two areas  that got us to where we are today  well i think it's by a certain set of  events a gentleman by the name of  vladimir putin  came to the  to the ascended to the top of the  russian leadership and rebuilt  a  basically single world a single leader  authoritarian or  you know dictatorship where he calls all  the shots and he has evolved a kind of a  two  two major sort of ideas in his  government one is that the collapse of  the soviet union was a was a bad thing  and somehow has to be reversed and two  that ukrainians don't really exist that  their linguistic difference is their  distinct culture all those things are  not actually true that this is some sort  of a fake construction that was  concocted by the austro-hungarians in  the 19th century and it goes against the  trap the thread of ukrainian history for  a millennium  tell us a little bit more about the  ukrainian culture versus the russian  culture because i think it's something  that we don't really understand very  well in the us  yeah so the ukrainians are kind of like  the kurds they've rarely had a stable  state they had a period of statehood uh  kind of quasi statehood under cossack  headmans but they were also under the  polish lithuanian commonwealth they were  under austro-hungary they were under  russia they were under the  tatars so they've been under the ottoman  empire so they've had a number of rulers  and leaders whereas russia has primarily  been russia meaning that the current  configuration of russia has been  primarily russia so ukrainians have  grown up as much with western or central  european influence on their culture  including the catholic world uh as have  uh unlike the russians so there's a  there's a big distinction in the sense  because ukraine was uh and also there's  a kind of a tradition of democracy even  the cossack uh leaders of the 17th and  18th centuries they had they were  elected by councils of their uh of their  units of their military units that uh  and they were patrons of the arts and  they built churches and they built  museums and they endowed education  so there's a little bit of a different  development i would say there's more of  a european the influence of the latin  language the influence european culture  is more pronounced than it was in  development  it feels like i want to talk about other  countries getting involved it feels as  though germany is trying to do a little  bit of a switzerland vibe and be a  little bit more  waffling a bit on its involvement if  germany continues to not get as involved  what does that mean for german relations  with the us and for other european  nations yeah well germany you know the  us is trying to put germany on side  president uh  biden made a major concession to germany  by  deciding not to impose sanctions on the  north stream uh gas pipeline i actually  do advise the ukrainian oil and gas  companies i want to declare that  interest but the fact is that that was a  gesture not to the russians but to  angela merkel and to the germans in the  hope that they would be more  accommodating in the face of putin's  aggressive recent aggressiveness that  hasn't really uh quite  worked out i do think there is some  potential tension in in the uh in the in  the bilateral uh relationship but i  don't think it's it's over i think one  of the issues that has to be understood  is that germany thinks that  it invaded russia as the soviet union  but in fact six million ukrainians died  the largest proportion of people who  died in the second world war as a result  of nazi aggression where of course jews  but ukrainians and belarusians came  second and third proportionally they  wiped out six million souls on the on  the territory what today's is ukraine  and uh but the germans seem to feel that  they owe the russians some uh  currency and i think that they equally  owe the ukrainian some currency and  unfortunately russian soft power and  russian propaganda has built this image  in the minds of many germans and i think  it's now important to kind of break down  that image and get a more realistic view  of what german obligations are to its  neighbors to the east  i want to go back to what you said about  how you consult for ukrainian energy  companies because there's a huge role  here for natural gas and and the fact  that russia supplies so much of it to  germany the biden administration has  already been making alternative plans in  case russia does cut off fuel sales to  other countries  and i wonder  what would be the catalyst for that  happening and from where you sit what  would need to happen  already was pinching on the spot markets  was pinching europe and reducing uh  supplies uh keeping the supplies from  the existing pipelines to 50 of their  capacity or even less  secondly russia could if tensions  be became even more pronounced could  really squeeze the caps and uh i think  they're generally short of a war and the  imposition of new sanctions i think  russia would more or less be playing  with the spot markets rather and with uh  on which partially germany is reliant i  mean germany and mrs merkel made them  made the decision to wean themselves off  of the nuclear power and that made them  much more dependent on russian natural  gas and i think they also rejected the  idea of building lng capacity because  they thought it would be too expensive  and not worth it uh and i think poland  and other countries that understood the  geopolitical threat diversified and have  more inputs by which to import uh  natural gas and i think this is an  opportunity for u.s business uh for the  lng sector to ramp up and to  strategically meet the demands ukraine  and europe have large storage capacities  and creating a strategic reserve of gas  to allow for these kinds of  periods of tension i think is the next  item on the agenda  big picture here in our final minute  together adrian a lot of experts we've  had on the show have said that calling  this a potential world war ii event is  going to bridge too far how would you  describe it  well i don't think it's a potential  world war ii event but it is you know  one of the great benefits of the  collapse of the soviet union was a kind  of a of a kind of a peace dividend at  least in the in the european theater and  i think that that peace dividend if  ukraine were to be somehow pacified and  reoccupied by russia in a very bloody  war apart from the many years of  instability that would throw into europe  europe and european markets would create  a permanent  reviving superpower a military  superpower that would have a much more  aggressive stance russia has demographic  problems with its  european population a big rise in the  muslim and turkic population and i think  they want to have a bigger slavic core  and i think that's part of their uh  strategy and if they were able if putin  succeeded we have a brand new kind of a  new soviet union uh with more market uh  oomph and  a firmer command of of energy resources  and i think it would be a huge problem  going forward for europe and for the us  it would be a major strategic challenge  that's why you're so important yeah  counterweight really to uh the western  world then adrian thank you so much  adrian karanicki is board member of the  ukrainian institute of america he is  also editor of american purpose

